From b4d1295e33aa7639fd9460f4a377fcd9128526d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 04:04:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] Frame packing on nvidia-drm through the driver's
 dumb buffers

Frame packing on NVIDIA needs the frame in a buffer that NVKMS scans
out: nvidia-drm gives a framebuffer an NVKMS surface only where its
memory is video memory (nvidia-drm-fb.c, nv_drm_framebuffer_init()).
The window's buffer of a frame-packed mode is not (the kernel says "has
no NVKMS surface: its memory is not video memory"), and the buffers of
Kodi's own that were to replace it could not be made: NVIDIA's GBM
refuses a linear buffer for scanout and rendering ("GBM could not
allocate a 2048x2205 buffer of AR24 for scanout and rendering (Invalid
argument)", RTX 5050, driver 620.73.04). So the mode was refused and
the output fell back to side by side.

nvidia-drm makes a dumb buffer pitch linear, for scanout, in video
memory on a card that has it (nvidia-drm-gem-nvkms-memory.c,
nv_drm_dumb_create()). Where GBM's own buffers could not be made, or
were not all taken in a test commit of the mode, Kodi now makes three
dumb buffers of the window's size and format (8 bits), on its DRM file:
each is given a dma-buf, its handle is closed at once (the dma-buf
holds the memory) and it is imported into GBM by that dma-buf at the
driver's pitch, as DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR, so that the buffer of GBM
holds the only handle of it and gbm_bo_destroy() closes nothing else.
Each buffer is checked and asked in a test commit as GBM's are.

The frame is drawn upright into a framebuffer object and flipped by the
GPU into the buffer, as before, where the EGL of the driver takes the
buffer as a render target (8-bit values written and read back). Where it
does not, the frame is read back (as BGRA where GL_EXT_read_format_bgra
is there) and copied by the processor into the mapped buffer, flipped,
red and blue where the format lays them; that copy is checked the same
way before use, and what it costs a frame is said in kodi.log after 240
frames. GBM's own buffers that EGL cannot draw into are refused as
before: only dumb buffers are copied into.

kodi.log says which buffers went out, how the frames reach them and, if
nothing could be made, what failed of GBM's and of the dumb buffers.
Nothing changes in any other mode, with any other driver, or where the
window's buffer is taken.
